WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.952
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:46.460
Good afternoon, everybody,
and welcome to today's Wiesner

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:47.960
gallery online tour.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.460
My name is Shannon
Rose McAuliffe,

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:51.460
and I'm the manager of
the student arts programs

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:52.580
here at MIT.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:54.680
I'm joined by
Sarah Hirzel, she's

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.080
the coordinator of the
Wiesner student art gallery

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:00.350
and Elaheh Ahmadi, who is
an interdisciplinary artist

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.020
from Tehran, Iran.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:02.520
[INAUDIBLE]

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.840
Elaheh is a 2020 graduate
of MIT in the Department

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.930
of Electrical Engineering
and Computer Science.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:10.850
And she's currently
a graduate student

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.710
in that very same program.

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:15.050
She learned photography as
an undergraduate student

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:18.260
in the Department of arts,
culture, and technology.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.330
Elaheh's project
has been sponsored

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:23.750
by the Council for the Arts
at MIT, also known as CAMIT.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:26.930
And after a brief
introduction by Sarah Herzl

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.360
about the exhibition,
Elaheh will lead us

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.940
through the virtual show
via her screen share.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.070
You're welcome to
view the exhibition

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:35.900
on her website, which
is going to be put

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.530
into the chat on your Sidebar.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.690
After this tour please
feel free to join us

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:42.350
for some question and answers.

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:45.070
Feel free to submit your
questions using the chat box,

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:47.440
use the Raise Hand
function and we'll

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.680
open the floor for some
really lovely discussion.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.980
And without further
ado, before we begin,

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.140
my friend Sarah has
a couple of words.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.810
Thank you so much, Shannon
Rose, for everything.

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:02.380
It has been a sincere
pleasure working with Elaheh

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:03.910
on this exhibition.

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:06.370
I want to extend my
particular thanks

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:09.430
to Heidi Erickson, our graphic
designer and web creator

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.740
for MIT'S office of the arts.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:14.170
We are grateful for
the generous support

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:16.510
of many members of the
staff of the Office

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:19.100
of the arts and CAMIT.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:21.650
Before we begin, I
want to acknowledge

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.280
how Elaheh beautifully
demonstrates the importance

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:27.800
of the arts at MIT.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.170
Her subjects are
very representative

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:33.600
of the creativity and
depth of our community.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.690
Elaheh is not just
a photographer.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.170
She is something
of a documentarian

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:43.670
and a social artist for whom
interpersonal connection

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:46.340
has high creative value.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:49.520
She invites her participants
and her audience

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.270
to bring their full selves into
the communities they study,

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:55.610
live, and play in.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:58.400
Nurturing the success
of women and others

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.090
in settings such as
MIT is important work.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:06.530
And it's exciting that Elaheh
can use the art of photography

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:11.090
to tell stories that allow
for openness and connection.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:14.660
She uses her own challenges
to nurture others.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:17.240
And as many of her
collaborators will attest,

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:19.310
she is a joy to work with.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:22.550
I am very pleased to
introduce Elaheh Ahmadi.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.540
Woo hoo!

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:32.070
Thank you, Sarah and Shannon
for the amazing introduction

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:34.520
and organizing this event.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:38.280
I'm so happy this is
finally happening.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.010
I'm so excited that all
of you could join us

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:43.620
for the opening of
this exhibition.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:46.380
This project has been
a huge part of my life

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.680
for the past three years and
I'm so excited to finally share

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.260
the result with all of you.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:56.430
I have put together a video
about the journey of Trapped

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:59.290
and I don't want
to spoil the rest.

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:02.901
So let's just watch it,
I hope you all enjoy it.

00:04:02.901 --> 00:04:06.268
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:11.380
Welcome to Trapped
exhibition, virtually

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:15.580
hosted by Wiesner
gallery at MIT.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.880
My name is a Elaheh and I
am the director of Trapped.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.920
This project
started in fall 2018

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:29.040
as a final project for my
intro to photography class,

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.380
taught by Laura [INAUDIBLE].

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:34.080
At that time, I was
dealing with a lot

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:35.700
of challenges in
my personal life

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.960
that led me to a
very dark place.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:42.060
But I found photography and
darkroom to be my saviors.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.990
In the class, Trapped started
with me expressing how

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.300
I was feeling at the moment.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:51.420
Trapped by all the
society's expectations.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:55.590
All the laws that kept
me from seeing my family.

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:59.160
The pressure of being
a female in engineering

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:02.970
and having to suppress
my femininity.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:06.390
And being trapped in a
relationship that getting out

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:08.920
of it meant being a failure.

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:11.910
So I asked a friend
or two to be my model

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:15.030
and help me capture my feelings.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:17.400
I showed the first couple
of photos to Laura,

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:22.150
and she told me, this doesn't
have to be just about you.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:24.120
It can be about all women.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:28.880
I'll give you another week
and go see where this goes.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:34.720
I am so grateful she said that
because if it wasn't for her,

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.350
I wouldn't have had the courage
to ask people to take time out

00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:40.900
of their lives to
just model for me.

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:46.490
But I gave it a shot, I started
opening up to my friends,

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:49.500
telling them how I
was feeling and little

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:54.050
did I know that I wasn't alone.

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:58.850
From my friends back home, to my
close friends and peers at MIT.

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:02.660
To people I just met
at a bar or party.

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:07.170
We all felt trapped,
one way or another.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:10.070
And it was then
that I realized, I

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:15.860
need to let people know
how we, women, are feeling.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:19.130
I need to take a
step into creating

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:22.805
a safe space for everyone
to be able to share

00:06:22.805 --> 00:06:24.080
their experiences.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:28.220
And for others to ask
questions about it.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:33.440
I believe that talking about the
issues and letting others know

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:39.110
is a good step toward creating
a society that treats everyone

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:40.650
equally.

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:42.230
So I did.

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:46.850
I made Trapped to be
the voice of all women

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:48.110
from all backgrounds.

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:54.350
The process would usually
start with a conversation

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:55.460
in some setting.

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:58.580
And someone would either
show interest in the project

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:01.830
or I would think of an
idea or two for the person.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:04.820
And I would propose them
to collaborate with me.

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:07.970
Before each photo shoot, I
would tell my participant

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:12.560
to wear whatever they want,
it just has to be all black.

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:17.360
And then after what I would call
a heart to heart conversation,

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:20.900
about our challenges, the
photo shoot would happen.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:23.360
Each photo shoot was unique.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:26.720
Sometimes I would
find a place on campus

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:31.280
or I would go to their houses
or we would just go to streets.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:34.040
And sometimes it just
happened at the moment.

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:38.730
And suddenly I had
the perfect shot.

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:42.520
But the photo shoot
wasn't just about that.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:45.660
It was about the
whole experience.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:49.590
Me, letting myself to be
vulnerable around my female

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:51.580
and non binary peers.

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:55.710
Them appreciating my
honesty and also opening up

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:57.710
about their experience.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:01.650
And us connecting
on a deeper level.

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:04.520
And then we would throw
ideas off of each other

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:06.230
until we were happy with a shot.

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:14.000
Once the photo
shoot was over, it

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:16.910
was time for
post-processing the images.

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:19.940
I use both digital
and 35 millimeter

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:21.890
film to shoot the photos.

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:25.370
I would spend hours in
a darkroom developing

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:28.220
and printing the photos
or in the digital lab

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:31.560
doing some minor edits
and printing the photos.

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:35.120
One of the most rewarding
parts of the process

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:39.812
was bringing the moments I had
with the participants to life

00:08:39.812 --> 00:08:40.604
through the photos.

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:49.230
This project not only helped me
to get through the hard times,

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:51.780
but it also helped many
of the participants

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:56.400
to start being more
vocal and expressive

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.570
with the challenges they're
dealing with on a daily basis.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:04.890
One of my participants posted
the photos on her Instagram

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:07.920
and she came out to
her family and friends

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:11.370
about her open
marriage and sexuality.

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:16.350
Which it made me
so happy and proud

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:19.890
to get to be apart of that.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:23.610
The project grew not
because I was pushing it,

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:26.580
but because our voices
through the photos

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:33.630
were so powerful that it
made people feel safe to open

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:35.310
up and talk about it.

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:37.560
All of it.

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:43.350
How our communities we grew up
in suffered from gender bias.

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:47.160
How gender bias affected
our life choices.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:51.300
How we felt shut down in
meetings and projects.

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:55.755
How we felt judged by the
way we dress or by our body.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.920
After 16 unique photo
shoots with different women,

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:09.690
I decided in February
2020 that I was

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:11.850
ready to wrap up the project.

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:15.960
So I called in
all the people who

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:19.500
participated before, all the
people who showed interest

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:24.120
at any time, for a
final group photo shoot.

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:27.570
I gave them the same
instructions, told all of them

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:29.723
that you need to
wear something black,

00:10:29.723 --> 00:10:31.140
whatever you are
comfortable with.

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:34.560
Put on as much makeup as you
want or don't, do your hair

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:37.290
or don't, just show
up with something

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:41.370
that you're comfortable with,
something that is in you.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:46.500
So I told everybody to
show up at 10 AM Saturday.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:49.710
This was different from
all the photo shoots

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:52.500
that I did before because
it wasn't a one on one.

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.770
Something that I was
like comfortable with

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:58.560
and it was a comfortable
intimate setup.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:03.150
It was a group
setting that everybody

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:06.810
from different backgrounds,
I asked all of them

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:08.700
to get together.

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:10.800
So before that all
of the photo shoots,

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:13.380
I didn't planned
them much beforehand.

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.640
Because I knew once
we get into it,

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:18.120
we will throw ideas
off of each other

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:19.770
and the photo shoot
would just happen.

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:21.490
But this one was different.

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:26.280
So what I did was I took all
of the prints that I had,

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:28.920
my camera, all the
set up, just showed up

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:30.540
where it was
supposed to show up.

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:34.920
And you should have been there.

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:37.170
It was amazing.

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:46.890
Just seeing all these awesome,
beautiful, smart, strong women

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:51.420
get together in this set.

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:54.720
And most of them didn't
even know other people.

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:57.570
Maybe every person knew
one or two other person.

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:04.110
But for a lot of them It
was a an uncomfortable setup

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:09.090
too because nobody did
modeling professionally.

00:12:09.090 --> 00:12:11.430
It was something
new for all of them.

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:19.020
But we all shared one thing, and
it was we believed in Trapped.

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:22.470
We believed that
Trapped is something

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:26.020
that can help all of
us voice our feelings,

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:33.750
our struggles and that was
what brought everybody together

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:34.530
on that day.

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:36.735
And it was amazing.

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:45.480
So at the beginning
of the session,

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:48.570
I put all the prints
down and I told

00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:54.810
everybody take whatever
print that you feel close to

00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:56.910
and write something
on the back of it

00:12:56.910 --> 00:13:00.450
or draw something or do
whatever you feel comfortable.

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:03.810
Something like if that
photo spoke to you

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:07.800
and that's why you picked it,
then you have something to say.

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:12.030
And that was for me to just get
everybody in that head space

00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:16.770
and also have some time
to put prepare the setup.

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:23.020
And the results
were just fabulous.

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:29.890
To see how other
people felt something

00:13:29.890 --> 00:13:35.620
with somebody else, who
maybe was from another world,

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:38.410
was born somewhere else,
had a completely different

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:42.760
background, but there was
still one connection there.

00:13:42.760 --> 00:13:46.840
In my opinion that was the
best way to warm up everybody.

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:50.170
Let everybody to be
comfortable, loosen up,

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:53.920
and get to that head space.

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:55.900
And we started shooting.

00:13:55.900 --> 00:13:58.990
We started taking
the group photos.

00:13:58.990 --> 00:14:03.070
You can see them now
in the exhibition.

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:03.950
I love them.

00:14:03.950 --> 00:14:07.720
I mean I'm biased, but I
think they look amazing.

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:13.090
Just seeing everybody's
beautiful faces,

00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:16.690
the different outfits, the
different way that they

00:14:16.690 --> 00:14:18.340
put themselves in the frame.

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:20.120
I didn't tell them what to do.

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:22.420
All I told them was exist.

00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:24.380
Do whatever feels
comfortable for you.

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:27.930
If that means talking with
somebody else that you know,

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:29.260
do that.

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:30.200
I don't care.

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:34.960
I want you to be in the
head space you are now

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:39.190
and express yourself
somehow with your body.

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:44.940
And I couldn't believe it
when I looked at my watch

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:49.415
and it was four PM and I was
shooting six hours nonstop.

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:54.660
I think for me that was one
of the best days I've ever

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:57.660
had doing anything.

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:01.290
Six hours not even
realizing how time passed.

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:05.070
And I was happy, just jumping
everywhere and looking

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:09.010
at the photos, loving everything
that was happening there.

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:12.570
And it was amazing
to see I wasn't

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:14.070
the only one enjoying myself.

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:16.620
Other people were
also really enjoying

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:20.520
talking to other people, getting
each other's contact info,

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:24.780
just connecting
and networking too.

00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:33.060
And it was the best way
to wrap up this project

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:37.960
because that was what I wanted.

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:40.800
I wanted to bring
people together.

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:44.310
I wanted to create a safe space.

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:50.640
And I wanted people
to see other women,

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:57.180
realize they're not alone,
and be proud of the struggles

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:00.810
and how far they came.

00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:05.940
And even though most of the
times everything is against us.

00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:12.640
Hi, I'm Alexa.

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:15.880
I'm from San Francisco and
I graduated MIT last year

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:17.380
in Core 6-2.

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:20.020
I'm currently a program
manager in camera and depth

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:22.030
hardware in the Bay Area.

00:16:22.030 --> 00:16:26.440
I had the honor and privilege of
doing a photo shoot for Trapped

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:28.360
with Elaheh.

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:32.360
The photo shoot was such
an awesome experience.

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:36.730
It was a glimpse into the crazy,
creative mind that is Elaheh.

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:39.250
We were just bouncing
ideas off of each other.

00:16:39.250 --> 00:16:41.080
What if you blur
this with motion?

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:43.807
What if you put this
pattern on your face?

00:16:43.807 --> 00:16:44.890
What if you turned around?

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:47.050
It was all these what
ifs flying around

00:16:47.050 --> 00:16:49.510
and I think part of the
reason it was so easy

00:16:49.510 --> 00:16:51.790
and fun is because she's
my really good friend

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:55.340
and it was a really comfortable
space for experimentation.

00:16:55.340 --> 00:16:57.520
And I think the other part
of it is because she's

00:16:57.520 --> 00:16:59.090
such a great photographer.

00:16:59.090 --> 00:17:04.540
And she turns the world into
her art so effortlessly.

00:17:04.540 --> 00:17:09.369
I think Trapped as
a whole is a set

00:17:09.369 --> 00:17:11.710
of feelings and emotions to me.

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:15.550
It's isolation, despair,
and hopelessness.

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:19.150
It's this feeling of being
where you don't want to be

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:21.099
and not knowing how to get out.

00:17:21.099 --> 00:17:23.140
I think that's
somewhere that we've all

00:17:23.140 --> 00:17:25.359
been to a certain extent.

00:17:25.359 --> 00:17:28.930
I think that's also the reason
why this project expanded

00:17:28.930 --> 00:17:30.490
to what it is now.

00:17:30.490 --> 00:17:34.540
It's because the theme is so
universal yet also so personal

00:17:34.540 --> 00:17:36.160
to each individual.

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:38.560
Thanks Elaheh for letting
me be a part of this

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:40.870
and I hope everyone enjoys
it as much as I did.

00:17:43.930 --> 00:17:45.310
Hi, my name is Ellie.

00:17:45.310 --> 00:17:47.410
I was born in Iran
and raised in the US.

00:17:47.410 --> 00:17:49.420
I currently live in
Los Angeles, California

00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:52.930
and I graduated from
Boston University in 2020.

00:17:52.930 --> 00:17:54.520
I was part of the
Trapped photo shoot

00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:56.740
and I'm extremely grateful that
I was a part of the Trapped

00:17:56.740 --> 00:17:59.230
photo shoot because it just
means so many different things

00:17:59.230 --> 00:18:00.070
to me.

00:18:00.070 --> 00:18:03.910
As a young girl growing
up in Iran and the US

00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:06.190
and hearing from all
different types of people,

00:18:06.190 --> 00:18:10.330
how women should feel
and how they should look

00:18:10.330 --> 00:18:12.730
and how they should act
and how they should react.

00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:16.510
And then thinking that's not how
I look, that's not how I feel,

00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:19.960
that's not how I want to act
or react to certain situations.

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:22.840
And just this Trapped
project was a way for me

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:25.480
to be able to of break down
some of those stereotypes

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:29.500
and show how I am
and what I feel.

00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:32.470
The photo shooting process was
something completely new to me

00:18:32.470 --> 00:18:34.970
and Elaheh is an
amazing photographer.

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:37.780
And she made me feel so
comfortable the entire time.

00:18:37.780 --> 00:18:40.990
As someone who doesn't always
like how she looks in pictures

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:45.040
and always picks out her flaws,
the way that the pictures came

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:48.770
out and just the entire
process, it was amazing.

00:18:48.770 --> 00:18:52.120
And I just felt so comfortable
and confident and beautiful

00:18:52.120 --> 00:18:55.480
in those pictures even
that's not how I always feel.

00:18:55.480 --> 00:18:58.420
The final photo shoot was
definitely my favorite.

00:18:58.420 --> 00:19:01.960
Seeing this beautiful
group of women

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:05.830
of all different backgrounds
and ages and cultures

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:08.800
coming together as the
culmination of this Trapped

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:09.970
project.

00:19:09.970 --> 00:19:12.100
And just seeing what
Trapped meant to them,

00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:14.650
and hearing their stories,
and their backgrounds.

00:19:14.650 --> 00:19:19.690
And sharing my story it was
just so empowering and amazing.

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:21.670
And I am extremely
grateful to have

00:19:21.670 --> 00:19:23.110
been a part of this project.

00:19:23.110 --> 00:19:25.990
And it really does mean
a lot to a lot of people.

00:19:31.870 --> 00:19:38.500
And now today, I am so proud
to present you the Trapped

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:40.060
exhibition.

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:42.910
A project that
would have not been

00:19:42.910 --> 00:19:48.220
possible without
every single person

00:19:48.220 --> 00:19:53.960
supporting me, and pushing me,
motivating me to move forward.

00:19:53.960 --> 00:19:59.620
It was an honor to work with
all these amazing people who

00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:02.470
worked on this project with me.

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:06.880
And to create a project
that will hopefully

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:13.060
give some people courage
to speak up and be

00:20:13.060 --> 00:20:17.725
who they want to be and do
what their heart desires.

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:26.200
And now the moment we've all
been waiting for, or at least

00:20:26.200 --> 00:20:29.110
I have, is finally here.

00:20:29.110 --> 00:20:34.090
I would like to welcome you
to the exhibition of Trapped.

00:20:34.090 --> 00:20:37.990
The first thing you will see
as you walk into the exhibition

00:20:37.990 --> 00:20:44.680
is the photo of a person in
their most vulnerable position.

00:20:44.680 --> 00:20:48.160
Surrounded by a light
where there is nothing

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:51.400
but darkness at the outside.

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:54.310
I see this photo
as a great opener

00:20:54.310 --> 00:20:56.800
for what you're about
to experience as you

00:20:56.800 --> 00:20:58.060
walk through the exhibition.

